# One line-ending convention, enforced rather than hoped for.
#
# Until this file existed, which ending a file got depended on whichever tool last wrote
# it: core.autocrlf is false, so nothing normalised on the way in, and 17 committed text
# files had drifted to CRLF while the rest of the tree stayed LF.
#
# The drift is not cosmetic. When a tool rewrites a whole file — Python's write_text emits
# os.linesep, i.e. CRLF on Windows — every line changes, so a three-line edit arrives as a
# whole-file diff. During 0.6.0 one ~110-line change reported 663 added / 552 removed; in
# 0.6.3 a three-line edit reported 1,994. That makes review impossible and destroys git
# blame lineage. It happened four times between 0.6.0 and 0.6.3 and every one was caught by
# chance, which is the argument for a rule: the tools reintroduce the problem faster than
# review catches it.
#
# text=auto lets git decide what is text and stores all of it with LF; eol=lf checks it
# back out as LF, so the working tree matches the repository on every platform.
* text=auto eol=lf

# cmd.exe is the one consumer that genuinely wants CRLF. Still stored as LF like everything
# else — only the checkout differs — so this rule adds no diff today. The .bat files here
# are three-line launchers that work either way; pinning it makes the guarantee explicit
# rather than dependent on them staying that simple.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Never convert these. Git's auto-detection already classifies them correctly, but stating
# it means a corrupted icon can never be the result of line-ending conversion.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
